I have the following yargs setup for one of my projects:
```ts
export const commands = yargs()
  .command(
    "help",
    "Show help menu for commands",
    () => {} // no args
  )

  .command(
    "exit",
    "Disconnect from the world",
    () => {} // no args
  )

  .command(
    "where",
    "Get current location",
    () => {} // no args
  )

  .command("come", "Walk to the sender", () => {})

  .command("build <prompt>", "Build something based on a prompt", yargs => {
    return yargs.positional("prompt", {
      type: "string",
      describe: "Prompt describing the structure",
    });
  });
```

...and I have a function that parses a string (converted to argv with the help of another library) as a command, and throws an error if the parsing fails:

```ts
export function parseCommand(input: string) {
  return commands
    .exitProcess(false)
    .strictCommands()
    .fail(msg => {
      throw new Error(msg);
    })
    .parseSync(stringArgv(input));
}
```

I was testing this function when I noticed that when I gave it the nonexistent command `fly away`, the error was:
```
Error: Missing required argument: prompt
```
This is not the correct, message, right? The message should say that it's an unknown command, but instead it is interpreting it as the "build" command in my yargs instance. I also had .strictCommands() on (tried .strict() as well), so is this an issue? Or am I misunderstanding how the strict modes work?

