I need a script to accept an optional array of integers, e.g.:

```
$ script build 1 2 3 4
```

OR be used without them at all:

```
$ script build
```

Here is my yargs def:

```ts
import yargs from "yargs";
import { hideBin } from "yargs/helpers";

const parser = yargs(hideBin(process.argv))
  .demandCommand(1)
  .command(
    "build [ids..]",
    "Build units",
    (yargs) =>
      yargs.positional("ids", {
        describe: "ID of the unit to build",
        number: true,
        type: "number",
        demandOption: false,
        array: true,
      }),
    async (argv) => {
      console.log(argv.ids); // Prints [] instead of undefined when run as "script build"
    }
  );

(async () => {
  const argv = await parser.argv;
  process.exit(0);
})();

```

The problem is that `argv.ids` is always defined and equals to an empty array `[]` when no arguments are provided.
Yet, the TypeScript definition of `argv.ids` is `number[] | undefined` as it should be.

So this is either a TypeScript bug or a Yargs bug.

Versions:
`yargs`: 17.4.0
`@types/yargs`: 17.0.10

Meanwhile the workaround is to replace the line:

```js
if (argv.ids != null) {
  ...
```

with:
```js
if (Array.isArray(argv.ids) && argv.ids.length > 0) {
  ...
```

