Hey folks! Since a wrote this issue I've [released `5.x.x` of Calibre's command line client](https://www.npmjs.com/package/calibre), which gained the ability to output documentation for commands.

In the interest of helping the yargs community, I'll attempt to describe how it works here in this post. If there's any questions or feedback, please feel free to share it here.

Links and references: 

* [Command Line Client Repository](https://github.com/calibreapp/cli/)
* [Command Line Client Command Documentation (HTML)](https://calibreapp.com/docs/automation/cli-commands)
* [Command Line Client Command Documentation (Markdown)](https://github.com/calibreapp/cli/blob/main/CLI_COMMANDS.md)

For CLI, I decided to update to ESM, then use esbuild to create a cjs bundle for ongoing support. 

As part of this migration effort, I realised that it would be no longer possible to use the [`commandDir` yargs function, as it doesn't currently support ESM. ](https://github.com/yargs/yargs/blob/main/docs/advanced.md#commanddirdirectory-opts)

However, I noticed the [Providing a command module section](https://github.com/yargs/yargs/blob/main/docs/advanced.md#providing-a-command-module) of the advanced topics yargs documentation. 

With a little re-arranging, I [updated our top level yargs entrypoint to accept an array of commands](https://github.com/calibreapp/cli/blob/main/src/cli.js#L16-L20), and updated each sub command to export it's properties: 

```js
const command = 'connection-list'
const describe = 'List all available network connection speeds.'
const handler = main
const builder = {
  json: options.json
}

export { command, describe, handler, builder }
```

Using this convention we were able to update each command and subcommand to have the same export options. I then wrote [`cli-metadata.js`](https://github.com/calibreapp/cli/blob/main/src/cli-metadata.js) `getCommandMetaData`, which:

* fetches the commands (from `cli-commands.js`)
* collects the exports, omitting the `builder` function (if present for a given command)
* returns a tree of command metadata, featuring the: `command`, `description`, `options` and `subcommands` of each command

<details>
<summary>The output, looks like this</summary>

```json
[
  {
    "command": "site <command>",
    "options": {},
    "subcommands": [
      {
        "command": "create <name> [options]",
        "describe": "Add a Site for Calibre to monitor.",
        "options": {
          "url": {
            "demandOption": true,
            "requiresArg": true,
            "describe": "The URL of the Site."
          },
          "location": {
            "demandOption": true,
            "requiresArg": true,
            "describe": "Choose the location for the test."
          },
          "team": {
            "describe": "The identifying slug of the Team."
          },
          "schedule": {
            "describe": "Set the schedule for automated Snapshots. Available options: hourly, daily, every_x_hours.",
            "default": "every_x_hours"
          },
          "interval": {
            "describe": "Set the Snapshot interval. Provide UTC hour (between 0 and 23) for daily Snapshots and numeric hour interval for every_x_hours option (between 1 and 168 hours).",
            "default": 6
          },
          "json": {
            "describe": "Outputs the results of the command in JSON format."
          }
        },
        "subcommands": []
      }]
     // SNIP
  }
]
```
</details>

To make the command metadata available, I added a named export to `package.json`:

```json
"exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./index.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    },
    "./cli-commands": "./src/cli-commands.js",
    "./cli-metadata": "./src/cli-metadata.js"
  },
```

As well as an npm script to construct markdown from the command metadata: `"generate-cli-md": "node ./generate-cli-md.js > CLI_COMMANDS.md"`. Calling `npm run generate-cli-md` saves the output from [`generate-cli-md.js`](https://github.com/calibreapp/cli/blob/main/generate-cli-md.js) to markdown.

In our documentation website, we imported the metadata retrieval function (`import { getCommandMetaData } from 'calibre/cli-metadata'`) and wrote a template that would format all the options accordingly. [Here's that page.](https://calibreapp.com/docs/automation/cli-commands)

Overall, this work led to a more straightforward authoring experience for writing and testing commands, and also enabled us to generate documentation without a lot of further effort. 

**Feedback for yargs authors**

At present, yargs only uses `command`, `aliases`, `describe`, `builder`, `handler` and `deprecated` exports from modules, but I found it to be far more ergonomic rather than chaining a yargs contructor method. I think yargs would be easier to use if it took a default position of suggesting module exports as a way to organise a CLI project. 

If the module options were expanded to the full yargs API (e.g: `examples`, `help`, `epilogue`, etc etc) I think it'd be hugely beneficial. I'd especially like to see `examples` becoming available, because it'd improve our documentation to be able to provide a command example and explanation of what it does! 

I hope my notes here have been helpful. If there's anything else I can help to provide, please let me know.

