```

The 1st issue is fixed. One needs to 
1) pass `true` as the second argument of `middleware()` - `applyBeforeValidation` 
2) AND set `required: true` in the `baz` option definition.

So the final code is:

```ts

import yargs from "yargs";

const input = ["cmd1", "-f", "Hello", "-b", "world"];

yargs(input)
  .command(
    "cmd1",
    "cmd1 desc",
    (yargs) =>
      yargs
        .option("foo", { type: "string", alias: "f", required: true })
        .option("bar", { type: "string", alias: "b", required: true })
        .option("baz", { type: "string", required: true, hidden: true })
        .middleware((argv) => {
          const { foo, bar } = argv;
          argv.baz = `${foo} ${bar}`;
        }, true),
    (argv) => {
      const { baz } = argv;
             //^? string | undefined
      console.log({ baz });
    }
  )
  .strict()
  .parse();

```

So the only problem so far is that artificial `baz` option declared, which user can set still.

@gluxon thank you for the bug report. It's my expectation that the following logic should ensure that `stdout`/`stderr` drains before an application exits:

https://github.com/yargs/yargs/blob/main/lib/utils/set-blocking.ts

This makes me think that the bug may be in the Node.js runtime itself.

I think this might be related, I'm experiencing an issue with `jest` (heavy users of `yargs`) help being cut-out but only in the following edge case:

```sh
yarn add jest
```

Then:
```js
