Alternatively to `.env()` (or in addition to it), some CLIs make use of environment variables and it would be useful to document their usage with Yargs, similar to the `.options()` API (though only for documentation purposes):

```ts
yargs
  .environmentVariables({
    FOO: {
      describe: 'Some foo stuff',
      type: 'boolean',
    },
    BAR: {
      describe: 'How many bars',
      type: 'number',
      default: 3,
    },
  });
```

Resulting in the following `help` output:

```
my-cli

Options:
  --help, -h  Show help  [boolean]
  --version   Show version number  [boolean]

Environment variables:
  FOO  Some foo stuff  [boolean]
  BAR  How many bars  [number] [default: 3]
```

One useful thing for example is to document environment variables a CLI might benefit from, but indirectly through its dependencies. For example, I use it to document [`chalk`'s `FORCE_COLOR`](https://github.com/chalk/chalk#chalksupportscolor) variable, even though my program doesn't use it directly but conveniently supports.

Thoughts?

---

Until there is built-in support for this, I made myself a small (very limited) helper, which I'll share here if anyone is interested. I am doing that as part of `.epilogue()` (hence #1680), I couldn't find a better way to do that but open to suggestions:

```tsx
function printEnvironmentVariables(
  variables: Record<
    string,
    Pick<Options, 'describe' | 'type'> & { default?: number | string }
  >,
) {
  const pad = Math.max(...Object.keys(variables).map(({ length }) => length));

  return `Environment variables:
  ${Object.entries(variables)
    .map(([name, opts]) => {
      const metadata = [
        ...(opts.type ? [`[${opts.type}]`] : []),
        ...(opts.default ? [`[default: ${opts.default}]`] : []),
      ].join(' ');

      return [
        name.padEnd(pad),
        ...(opts.describe ? [opts.describe] : []),
        ...(metadata ? [metadata] : []),
      ].join('  ');
    })
    .join('\n  ')}`;
}
```

Used as:

```
yargs
  .epilogue(
    printEnvironmentVariables({
      FOO: {
        describe: 'Some foo stuff',
        type: 'boolean',
      },
      BAR: {
        describe: 'How many bars',
        type: 'number',
        default: 3,
      },
    })
  );
```

Obviously this supports very few things, but I just adapted it to my use case so far, I'll update the snippet above as I add more stuff.

