I've done a little more digging and think I know what's going on, we're simply not setting blocking mode in `set-blocking` when run through `nyc`.   Since there's not much in `set-blocking`...

``` javascript
module.exports = function (blocking) {
  [process.stdout, process.stderr].forEach(function (stream) {
    if (stream._handle && stream.isTTY && typeof stream._handle.setBlocking === 'function') {
      stream._handle.setBlocking(blocking)
    }
  })
}
```
Running `nyc --help` with a breakpoint in `set-blocking` on the `if` statement shows `stream.isTTY` to be `undefined` so it never sets blocking.  If I force `setBlocking` in `set-blocking` then the `nyc` tests run successfully.

@rtfpessoa the [issue](https://github.com/istanbuljs/nyc/issues/1259) I was seeing with nyc seems to be due to the way child processes work.  In one of nyc's tests it checks `stdout` output from a `nyc` instance running in a child process.  A child process creates `stdout` as a pipe, not a TTY connection, so the above code can't set blocking mode.

Anyway if you're trying to run yargs without a TTY connection then you may experience flakiness.

As far as fixes go, the simplest would be to drop the `stream.isTTY` test but I get the feeling that might break something else.  It looks like it's related to a long standing node [issue](https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/6456) and since there is currently no solution their advice is to exit gracefully and not use `process.exit`.

@AndrewFinlay unfortunately there are 1000s of programs in the wild that assume `--help` will terminate an application before the next line of code is reached.

If you structure your program appropriately `.exitProcess(false)`, I suppose, might help make sure that the whole buffer is drained before the application exits, but I don't think this is something we can switch to being the default behavior.

I'm actually wondering out loud, is the problem that we should be calling [set-blocking](https://www.npmjs.com/package/set-blocking) in nyc? This bug seems to come about when draining stderr/stdout is allowed to be non-blocking.

