Here's another workaround that works for me.  The `yargs.parse()` method takes an optional callback argument.  If you specify a callback function, then Yargs will **not** display any output.  Instead, it passes the output as a string to the callback function.  You can then modify the string to remove the types.

```javascript
  function yargsCallback(err, args, output) {
    if (output) {
      // Remove types (e.g. [string], [boolean]) from the output
      output = output.replace(/\[\w+\]/g, '');
      
      // Show the modified output
      console.log(output);
    }
  }
